messages.body_plain:
Hi Stephan,

Not sure what I am doing wrong.

-------------------

I added:
rConfig.setJavaArgs(rConfig.getJavaArgs() + " " + "-Dde.walware.rj.debug");
to my code (
https://github.com/marekjagielski/R-from-Java/blob/master/src/main/java/com/systemincloud/rfromjava/Main.java)
and not saw output in Console.

I played also with:
rConfig.setEnableVerbose(true);

What I was able to see is output logs in out.log file.

-------------------

I started to play with your code. I added
to de.walware.rj.servi.internal.LocalNodeFactory

pBuilder.inheritIO();

into method: void createNode(final NodeHandler poolObj) throws RjException

and yes - in this case I started to see prints in output console. However
the logs like:

[rmi://127.0.0.1:51235/pool-1461620214094, -embedded, -verbose]
Apr 25, 2016 11:36:54 PM de.walware.rj.server.srvImpl.AbstractServerControl
initVerbose
CONFIG: verbose mode enabled.
Apr 25, 2016 11:36:54 PM de.walware.rj.server.srvImpl.AbstractServerControl
initVerbose
CONFIG: java properties:
java.runtime.name=Java(TM) SE Runtime Environment
sun.boot.library.path=/usr/lib/jvm/java-7-oracle/jre/lib/amd64
java.vm.version=24.80-b11
de.walware.rj.debug=
            ...

Still I couldn't see outputs of method 'cat'

-------------------
What was promising - the output:

R version 3.2.4 (2016-03-10) -- "Very Secure Dishes"
Copyright (C) 2016 The R Foundation for Statistical Computing
Platform: x86_64-pc-linux-gnu (64-bit)
...

I found that this was generated by code
in de.walware.rj.servi.internal.NodeServer

final RjsComObject receivedCom =
NodeServer.this.internalEngine.runMainLoop(this.client, sendCom);
sendCom = null;
error = false;
if (receivedCom != null) {
switch (receivedCom.getComType()) {
case RjsComObject.T_PING:
sendCom = RjsStatus.OK_STATUS;
break;
case RjsComObject.T_MAIN_LIST:
MainCmdItem item = ((MainCmdS2CList) receivedCom).getItems();
MainCmdItem tmp;
ITER_ITEMS : for (; (item != null); tmp = item, item = item.next, tmp.next
= null) {
switch (item.getCmdType()) {
case MainCmdItem.T_CONSOLE_WRITE_ITEM:
this.out.println("R-OUT (" + item.getOp() + "): " + item.getDataText());
break;
case MainCmdItem.T_CONSOLE_READ_ITEM:
this.out.println("R-PROMPT: " + item.getDataText());
break;
}
}
break;

However, in case of methods like

this.fRservi.evalVoid("cat(\"Hello from R\")", null);

trace goes to de.walware.rj.server.client.AbstractRJComClient:
I added there similar code as above hoping to print evaluation:


receivedCom = this.rjConsoleServer.runMainLoop(sendCom);
this.mainC2SList.clear();
sendCom = null;
System.out.println("client *<- server: " + receivedCom);
switch (receivedCom.getComType()) {
case RjsComObject.T_PING:
ok = 0;
sendCom = RjsStatus.OK_STATUS;
continue;
case RjsComObject.T_MAIN_LIST:
System.out.println("T_MAIN_LIST");
MainCmdItem item = ((MainCmdS2CList) receivedCom).getItems();
System.out.println(item);
MainCmdItem tmp;
ITER_ITEMS : for (; (item != null); tmp = item, item = item.next, tmp.next
= null) {
System.out.println(item.getCmdType());
switch (item.getCmdType()) {
case MainCmdItem.T_CONSOLE_WRITE_ITEM:
System.out.println("R-OUT (" + item.getOp() + "): " + item.getDataText());
break;
case MainCmdItem.T_CONSOLE_READ_ITEM:
System.out.println("R-PROMPT: " + item.getDataText());
break;
}
}


As a result I see:

client *-> server: MainCmdC2SList ():
<ITEM i="0">
DataCmdItem EVAL_EXPR_VOID
options= 0x80000000
<SOURCE-EXPR>
cat("Hello from R")
</SOURCE-EXPR>
<DATA/>
<TARGET-EXPR/>
</ITEM>
client *<- server: MainCmdS2CList (isBusy=false)
T_MAIN_LIST
null

So it means that
 ((MainCmdS2CList) receivedCom).getItems();
has no items.


-------------------
Maybe I went too deep in details - it is interesting to look at your code.
But I am still far away to understand it.

To make you better view what I want to achieve: I will be running code of
my final user. User will define his code in classes like:

MyTask <- R6Class("MyTask",
  inherit = RTask,
  public = list(
    rtaskinfo = RTaskInfo$new(),
    in1 = NA,
    initialize_ports = function() {
      self$in1 <- InputPort$new(name = "in1", data_type = Bool)
    },
    initialize = function() {
      self$initialize_ports()
      cat("Init ")
    },
    execute = function(grp) {
      # this will run when all synchronous ports from group receive data
      cat("Executed")
    }
  ),
  private = list(
  )

I want that final user can print from methods for debug purpose.
Similar feature I made already for python:
https://github.com/systemincloud/sic-examples/tree/master/com.systemincloud.examples.tasks.pythontask
from where I can print using print("Hello from Python")

Thanks for help. I hope and see it's happening that our tools can work
nicely together.

Marek

